    <patterns>
      <pattern name="Bound-and-surface-Capped" location="src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Infrastructure/ExternalAccess/AccessibleRecordSetService.cs:780-960">
        The membership walk's existing NFR-03 treatment: a hard ceiling, a page ceiling, and a `Capped` flag plus `CapCeiling` carried out on the result so the caller can render the message without hard-coding the number. Copy this shape if the chosen fix is a bound rather than a batch.
      </pattern>
      <pattern name="Per-subject fail-closed read" location="src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Infrastructure/ExternalAccess/SubjectStandingGrantReader.cs:155-238">
        One read for both attributes, try/catch to `StandingGrantState.NotHeld`, and an observable warning when a secured attribute is absent from an otherwise-successful retrieve. A batched read must preserve the per-subject fail-closed value.
      </pattern>
    </patterns>
  </knowledge>

  <steps mode="directional">
    <step order="0">Re-measure N and record it. Count what the loop actually costs: distinct active org memberships per contact (the junction, `sprk_contactorganizations`, `statecode eq 0`), NOT organizations holding a standing grant. `notes/task-020-org-grant-spe-cleanup.md` §1 records 2 active membership rows across 2 organizations from live Dataverse on 2026-08-26 — treat that as a prior, re-derive if the environment is reachable, and state plainly which it is. Then decide the shape: one batched read, or an explicit bound with `Capped`. The choice is the escalation below.</step>
    <step order="1">Implement the chosen shape in the org-expansion term, preserving task 043's single junction read and the per-subject fail-closed value. Keep the bucketing by distinct baseline (`:1223-1228`) — that is what collapses the WALKS to at most three and is a separate, already-correct optimization.</step>
    <step order="2">Update the existing org-expansion tests. Two sites break by construction if the read is batched: the per-org Moq setup (`AccessibleRecordSetServiceTests.cs:1455`) and the `Times.Never` verification that no org read happens when the contact belongs to no organization (`:1493-1495`). The second is a real negative case — preserve its MEANING, do not delete it.</step>
    <step order="3">Add the tests in the closed set, including N greater than 1 and the partial-failure case. Run perturbations.</step>
    <step order="4">Build, full suite, ArchTests, publish size versus a fresh master worktree; write notes; report. Leave TASK-INDEX to the main session.</step>
  </steps>

  <escalation>
    <trigger>The SHAPE is a judgment call, and the register names two options without choosing: (a) one batched read for N organization ids, or (b) an explicit bound with `Capped` surfaced per NFR-03. They are not equivalent — (a) removes the cost and adds a method to a shared seam; (b) keeps N round trips but makes the ceiling honest, and on this path a cap means SILENTLY DROPPING organizations that confer access unless `Capped` is consumed by a caller that can act on it. Present both with a recommendation and the measured N before implementing. Do not pick silently.</trigger>
    <trigger>If the measurement shows N can exceed a few dozen for a real contact, say so and stop: that changes this from a shape cleanup into a correctness question about whether an unbounded additive term can be evaluated on the hot path at all, which is wider than this task.</trigger>
  </escalation>

  <justification>
    <existing>`ISubjectStandingGrantReader.ReadForOrganizationAsync` (per-subject, `SubjectStandingGrantReader.cs:141`), and the membership walk's `Capped` bound-and-surface machinery (`AccessibleRecordSetService.cs:780-960`).</existing>
    <extension>Both candidate shapes EXTEND what exists: a batched read is one method on the existing reader over the already-registered `IGenericEntityService.RetrieveMultipleAsync`; a bound reuses the existing `Capped`/`CapCeiling` fields. No new service, interface family, package or client.</extension>
    <cost-of-doing-nothing>Every authorization decision for a contact with M active org memberships pays M serial single-row Dataverse retrieves, and `ComposeAsync` has no cache, so the cost repeats per decision. Today those reads return `NotHeld` and contribute nothing — the round trips are pure waste on the hot path, and they grow with firm membership, which is operator-editable data rather than anything the code bounds.</cost-of-doing-nothing>
  </justification>

